Sculpt the **Skyshard Interceptor**, a symmetric forward-swept fighter jet, as a
24x8x32 opaque-voxel model using only the voxel tool.

## How sculpting works

The only way to place a voxel is the `voxel` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**,
and the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. A model produced
any other way (writing `voxels.json` directly, editing `model.png` by hand) is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no
transparency. After each call the binary re-renders the current model to an
isometric preview at `model.png` and appends the operation to `actions.json`. A
blank starting preview and an empty log are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
voxel fill-box --x0 10 --y0 2 --z0 4 --x1 13 --y1 5 --z1 28 --color "#8a94a6"
```

Run `voxel --help` to list every operation and `voxel <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

